WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:02.049
OK, so the next thing that we need

2
00:00:02.049 --> 00:00:06.580
to look at within the context of lightmaps and lightmaps,

3
00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.460
view modes and visualizers are going

4
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.509
to help us understand what's actually

5
00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:13.710
going on under the hood when it comes to performance

6
00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:14.710
optimization.

7
00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.289
So the categories that we're going

8
00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:17.870
to cover for the visualizers are

9
00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.210
going to be light complexity and lightmap density.

10
00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:21.670
Now, they're really easy to access.

11
00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:26.370
All you have to do is go to the View Mode tab

12
00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:29.710
and go to Optimization Modes and choose either of these.

13
00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:31.589
In the case of lightmap complexity,

14
00:00:31.589 --> 00:00:33.369
it's going to be View Mode, Optimization View

15
00:00:33.369 --> 00:00:35.820
Modes, and Light Complexity.

16
00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.780
It'll give you, essentially, a colored view,

17
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:42.060
as you can see here, of the scene that

18
00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:45.939
explains how complex a particular lighting setup is

19
00:00:45.939 --> 00:00:48.119
for a given pixel.

20
00:00:48.119 --> 00:00:51.539
So you'll notice that overlapping movable lights

21
00:00:51.539 --> 00:00:52.780
will be very expensive.

22
00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.780
They'll go closer to the pink-white range.

23
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.899
And you'll see the static lights are basically free.

24
00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:01.899
So bake your light wherever possible

25
00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:03.479
if you're using the lightmap solution,

26
00:01:03.479 --> 00:01:07.939
because it will be the cheapest way of rendering your scene.

27
00:01:07.939 --> 00:01:10.260
The next one is going to be lightmap density, which

28
00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.340
is going to be in the same spot just right below it.

29
00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:15.739
And it displays the density based on the tessellation

30
00:01:15.739 --> 00:01:17.019
display.

31
00:01:17.019 --> 00:01:21.299
Try and keep your lightmaps as low as possible.

32
00:01:21.299 --> 00:01:23.140
And essentially, this will help in general

33
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.219
with baking and optimization, baking times specifically.

34
00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:29.599
So let's go ahead and take a look at how this actually

35
00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:30.120
works.

36
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.959
So if I go into the editor with the project

37
00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:34.959
that you've been supplied for this,

38
00:01:34.959 --> 00:01:36.920
I've gone to my interior scene.

39
00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.840
So we're going to Light in 103.1, Interior,

40
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.750
and I've just opened the level.

41
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:47.840
Let's take a look at how we can see those optimization

42
00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:48.359
view modes.

43
00:01:48.359 --> 00:01:51.719
So I'm going to go to my View Mode tab,

44
00:01:51.719 --> 00:01:57.920
go to Optimization View Modes, and look at Light Complexity.

45
00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.420
So here, you can see the areas

46
00:01:59.459 --> 00:02:02.980
where light is basically fully baked, very, very little

47
00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.459
performance impact.

48
00:02:05.459 --> 00:02:09.419
But the more dynamic in certain areas the lights become

49
00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:10.780
and the more overlapping we have,

50
00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.219
the more expensive it becomes.

51
00:02:12.219 --> 00:02:13.580
Let's take a quick look at it.

52
00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.460
If I just add a light now without building

53
00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.840
and essentially calculate it as dynamic.

54
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:24.629
So I'll go to Lights, Point Light, and drag one in.

55
00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:25.990
And I set it as Moveable.

56
00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:28.729
So we're not technically needing to build this,

57
00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:31.860
but this is being calculated at runtime, right?

58
00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:38.030
So if I take a look at that lighting complexity again,

59
00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:40.830
you'll notice it starts to get really expensive.

60
00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:44.310
As I duplicate lights and have overlaps of those light

61
00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:47.710
radiuses, you'll see it grows exponentially almost.

62
00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:50.990
So something to keep in mind, again, bake whatever you can.

63
00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:53.349
But as I say, this View Mode here

64
00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:55.349
is going to help you a lot to understand

65
00:02:55.349 --> 00:02:57.509
why your frame rate might be dropping in certain areas

66
00:02:57.509 --> 00:02:59.370
due to lighting.

67
00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:02.189
So the next thing is going to be Optimization View Modes,

68
00:03:02.189 --> 00:03:03.069
Lightmap Density.

69
00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:05.030
And you can see here, it allows

70
00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:07.550
you to see the density of a particular mesh.

71
00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.389
Now, if a mesh, for example, was a little too high,

72
00:03:10.389 --> 00:03:14.449
for example, these small ones, it'll appear as red.

73
00:03:14.449 --> 00:03:16.009
And that's just down to the resolution

74
00:03:16.009 --> 00:03:20.169
that it has per size on the screen or density.

75
00:03:20.169 --> 00:03:23.050
So one thing you can do is you can overwrite

76
00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:24.729
the lightmap resolution.

77
00:03:24.729 --> 00:03:28.800
And let's say, for example, I set this to 1024.

78
00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.639
You can see it shows us much smaller little squares,

79
00:03:31.639 --> 00:03:33.680
and it starts to give us the information

80
00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.639
on the visualization mode here that it's

81
00:03:37.639 --> 00:03:39.840
a little higher than the rest of the scene around it.

82
00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.560
So that's something to keep in mind.

83
00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:43.360
One other thing to keep in mind with this

84
00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.479
is that it's better to have the general scene kind of have

85
00:03:46.479 --> 00:03:48.360
an overall similar tone.

86
00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:51.099
And the reason for this is if you have a very high resolution

87
00:03:51.099 --> 00:03:54.560
mesh here or lightmap resolution here compared

88
00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:55.939
to this one, you'll probably start

89
00:03:55.939 --> 00:03:58.479
to notice differences in the resolution of the shadows.

90
00:03:58.479 --> 00:04:00.060
And that's something to keep in mind

91
00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.020
because it can make things a little bit more noticeable

92
00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:03.860
in that sense.

93
00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.620
So try and keep things nice and consistent.

94
00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:08.699
And that's really it for visualization view modes

95
00:04:08.699 --> 00:04:09.699
in terms of lighting.

96
00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:13.039
Next, we'll take a look at IES profiles.
